<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhao Rugu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1170">1170</a>–<a href="/years/1231">1231</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Chau Ju-kua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhao Rugu was a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> imperial clan who served as superintendent of maritime trade at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>. About 1225 he wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-fan-zhi">Zhu Fan Zhi</a> (Description of Foreign Peoples), which describes the countries, ports and products of maritime Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> from merchants' reports. It is a key source for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a> of the thirteenth century.</p></div>
